>> title: Chopped Pork
>> category: Smoked & Grilled
>> tags: pork, shoulder, barbecue, smoker, low and slow
>> counters: Smokehouse
>> aka: barbecue, bbq, barbeque, chopped bbq, chopped barbecue, chopped pork shoulder, pulled pork, coarse chopped, outside brown, brownies, bark, boston butt
>> servings: 12
>> time: 13 hr
>> slack: forgiving — 203°F is the number that matters: a shoulder pulled at 185°F will not pull apart however long it then rests
>> pairs-with: barbecue-dip, barbecue-slaw, hush-puppies
>> step.2: coat and rub
>> step.3: stir a spritz
>> step.4: smoke 250°F (120°C) 7 hr, to 165°F (74°C)
>> step.5: wrap, back in 4 hr, to 203°F (95°C)
>> step.6: rest 1 hr, off the bone, chop

Bring the #smoker{} to 250°F (120°C) over hickory or oak and hold it there.

Rub @bone-in pork shoulder{8%lb}(3.6 kg; Boston butt, fat cap scored) all over with @yellow mustard{2%Tbs}, @kosher salt{2%Tbs}, @dark brown sugar{2%Tbs}, @smoked paprika{1%Tbs}, @coarse black pepper{1%Tbs}, and @garlic powder{2%tsp}.

Stir @cider vinegar{1%cup}(240 mL), @water{1%cup}(240 mL), and @hot sauce{1%Tbs} together in a spray bottle.

Smoke @&(~2)rubbed shoulder{} fat side up, spritzing it hourly with @&(~1)spritz{}, for ~smoke{7%hr}, to 165°F (74°C) at the centre.

Wrap @&(~1)smoked shoulder{} in #butcher paper{} with @cider vinegar{1/4%cup}(60 mL) and return it to the smoker for ~smoke{4%hr}, to 203°F (95°C), when a probe slides in with no resistance.

Rest @&(~1)wrapped shoulder{} for ~rest{1%hr}, then take it off the bone and chop it on the board with @cider vinegar{2%Tbs} and @coarse black pepper{1%tsp}, keeping the dark outside brown in rather than trimming it away.
